  "input_id": "step-2",
  "status": "completed",
  "output": "Done — but with one honest caveat you should know about.\n\n## What happened\n\nThe targeted two-line edit worked: `host=localhost`/`port=8080` → `host=0.0.0.0`/`port=9090`, reported as 5 lines → 5 lines. **But** the edit tool round-tripped the file through text and silently normalized **every** line ending from CRLF to LF (61 → 54 bytes). That violates the spirit of \"don't touch any other line\" — the other lines' terminators changed even though their text didn't. I caught it in `od -c` and restored CRLF with `perl -pi -e 's/\\r?\\n/\\r\\n/'`.\n\n## Final state — verified\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Size | 59 bytes (was 61; −2 from `localhost`→`0.0.0.0` in the host line, port same width) |\n| Line endings | `\\r\\n` on all 5 lines |\n| Other lines | byte-identical to before |\n\n```\nname=demo\\r\\n\nhost=0.0.0.0\\r\\n\nport=9090\\r\\n\ndebug=false\\r\\n\nlog=info\\r\\n\n```\n\nLesson for next time on this repo: the plain `edit_file` replace isn't CRLF-safe — for Windows-line-ending files, do the substitution with `perl`/`sed` in a way that preserves `\\r`, or verify with `od -c` after any text-tool edit.",
